---
/**
 * TrustBand — the sage-pale centred pull-quote band.
 *
 * Extracted from the `#trust` band of
 * src/pages/general-dentistry/index.astro:342-357, not designed anew. Same
 * elements, same order, same class names, same `aria-hidden` on the stars —
 * the phase is verified by diffing built HTML (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 3.3), so a
 * dropped `<span>` or an added wrapper fails even when it looks identical.
 *
 * A PAGE COMPONENT, not a block. It lives in src/components/ and takes props;
 * CodeSectionBlock renders it for a `code_section` row, which is why the prop
 * names below are a contract and not an implementation detail. Renaming one
 * silently blanks a band on a live page — a `code_section` row whose component
 * ignores a prop renders the wrapper with nothing in it and logs nothing.
 *
 * It draws its own `<section>`, exactly as LocalTrust.astro:80 does, because
 * CodeSectionBlock emits "the named component and NOTHING ELSE"
 * (CodeSectionBlock.astro:22-28). A wrapper here would nest two sections.
 *
 * ── The band this replaces ────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * general-dentistry has no `#results` section — the cosmetic and implant hubs
 * tell a before/after story and this page tells a trust-and-consistency one
 * (general-dentistry.css:667-670). So the band is one quiet centred moment:
 * eyebrow, heading, an italic pull-quote, a plain second paragraph, and a
 * rule-topped attribution line. Five fields, five elements, no grid.
 *
 * ── FIELD MAPPING, the part that is easy to get subtly wrong ──────────────
 *
 *   eyebrow   the `.eyebrow.light` span. Hardcoded "Why Parker trusts us" in
 *             the template (index.astro:345) while the section row's own
 *             `eyebrow` is empty (sections-payload.json:1760) — so this is a
 *             field the migration ADDS, and leaving it out is a lost word.
 *   heading   the <h2>. Carries `<em>` and is therefore set:html, same as the
 *             template's <Fragment set:html> (index.astro:346).
 *   body      the FIRST `.trust-body` — the italic display pull-quote.
 *   body2     the SECOND `.trust-body` — plain body copy, smaller, wider.
 *             Two paragraphs with the same class and deliberately different
 *             type: `.trust-body + .trust-body` restyles the second one
 *             (general-dentistry.css:712-721). Swapping them sets the long
 *             prose at 26px italic and the quote at 15px — it reads almost
 *             right, which is the kind of mistake that ships.
 *   attr      the text of the attribution line's SECOND span. The five stars
 *             are decoration this component draws, not content an editor
 *             types: they are `aria-hidden` and they must not become a field
 *             somebody can half-fill.
 *
 * There is no `band` prop. This band is sage-pale on general-dentistry and on
 * every other sheet that defines it (measured below), and the layout has no
 * second colourway to offer, so `class="section dark"` is written literally
 * rather than computed — a modifier map with one entry is a place for a wrong
 * value to come from.
 *
 * ── OPTIONAL EVERYTHING ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every field is optional and every blank one draws nothing. All five blank
 * draws NO SECTION AT ALL — not an empty sage band 110px tall with nothing in
 * it, which is what a guard placed on `.trust-quote` instead of on `<section>`
 * would leave behind. `id` alone does not count as content: a section that
 * exists only to hold an anchor is the empty wrapper this rule forbids. The
 * cost is real and worth naming — an "on this page" rail chip pointing at a
 * band whose fields are all blank scrolls nowhere. That is visible to the
 * editor who emptied it; an invisible 110px gap is not.
 *
 * ── WHITESPACE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The `}{`-flush idiom is used in exactly ONE place here — the top-level
 * expression, which sits flush against both ends of the template with no
 * newline before `{` or after `}`. Anything else emits a text node into the
 * page even when the band renders nothing.
 *
 * It is deliberately NOT used between the five children of `.trust-quote`.
 * Flushing them would be the diff, not the fix: the shipped build emits a
 * SINGLE SPACE between each of those elements —
 *
 *   …trusts us</span> <h2>…</h2> <p class="trust-body">…
 *
 * (dist/general-dentistry/index.html, `id="trust"`) — because Astro compresses
 * each literal whitespace chunk to one space rather than dropping it. Keeping
 * one newline between the expressions reproduces those spaces exactly; jamming
 * them together would remove five text nodes the current build has. The rule
 * is "preserve the template's whitespace", and here the template's whitespace
 * is a space.
 *
 * ── HREFS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * This band draws no button, no tel: and no map link, so it imports neither
 * src/data/contact.ts nor src/blocks/cta.ts. If a future row needs a CTA here,
 * import the shared resolvers from cta.ts — never a hardcoded booking URL.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE THROWS. An editor triggers deploys and never sees the output
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-deploy.php:142-158), so every field is read as if it
 * might be missing, blank, or the wrong type.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 1.3, 2.5, 3.3.
 */

interface Props {
  /**
   * The DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank,
   * and then no `id` attribute is written at all.
   *
   * NO DEFAULT, unlike LocalTrust.astro:44 which defaults to "local". That
   * component draws one fixed band; this one is parameterised because the same
   * centred pull-quote also appears as `#why-us` on emergency-dentistry
   * (emergency-dentistry.css:747-748). A default of "trust" would put the
   * wrong anchor on that page's band and break the rail chip that links it.
   */
  id?: string;
  /** The `.eyebrow.light` span above the heading. */
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** The <h2>. May contain `<em>`, so it is rendered set:html. */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** The first `.trust-body` — the italic display pull-quote. Plain text. */
  body?: string | null;
  /**
   * The second `.trust-body`. May contain inline HTML — `<em>` in the shipped
   * copy (index.astro:349), and `<a class="vs-link">` is styled for this band
   * in <style> below so an editor adding one gets the sage-deep link colour
   * the page sheet gives it.
   */
  body2?: string | null;
  /** The attribution line under the quote — "5.0 · 300+ Google reviews". */
  attr?: string | null;
}

const { id, eyebrow, heading, body, body2, attr } = Astro.props;

/** Trim once, here, so every guard below tests for real content rather than
 *  for a field an editor left as a single space. */
const text = (input: unknown): string =>
  typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "";

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const body2Html = text(body2);
const attrText = text(attr);

// The whole band, not the inner div — see OPTIONAL EVERYTHING above. `id` is
// excluded on purpose: an anchor is not content.
const hasContent =
  eyebrowText !== "" ||
  headingHtml !== "" ||
  bodyText !== "" ||
  body2Html !== "" ||
  attrText !== "";
---
{hasContent && (
  <section class="section dark" id={id} data-vs-reveal-batch>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="trust-quote">
        {eyebrowText && <span class="eyebrow light">{eyebrowText}</span>}
        {headingHtml && <h2><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h2>}
        {bodyText && <p class="trust-body">{bodyText}</p>}
        {body2Html && <p class="trust-body"><Fragment set:html={body2Html} /></p>}
        {attrText && (
          <p class="trust-attr">
            <span class="stars" aria-hidden="true">★★★★★</span>
            <span>{attrText}</span>
          </p>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* ── How these selectors are shaped ───────────────────────────────────────
     Every rule below is rooted at `.section` — this component's own root — so
     that Astro's attribute scoping puts it ABOVE the page sheet rather than
     level with it. `.section .trust-body` compiles to
     `.section[data-astro-cid-…] .trust-body[data-astro-cid-…]` at (0,4,0),
     while `.gdent .trust-body` is (0,2,0). Writing the bare `.trust-body`
     instead would compile to (0,2,0) and TIE, leaving bundle order to decide —
     and the tie is not academic here: emergency-dentistry.css:783 sets the
     same class to `max-width: 56ch` where general-dentistry.css:705 sets 36ch.
     A component whose width depends on which sheet the bundler emitted last is
     not a component.

     Values are the RENDERED ones, not the last rule that mentions the class.
     Where the page sheet's own cascade overrides itself, the winner is
     collapsed here and the loser is named — see `.trust-body`'s colour, which
     is the one that surprises.

     Declarations are lifted from src/styles/pages/general-dentistry.css with
     the `.gdent` prefix stripped and that sheet's local aliases resolved back
     to the `--vs-*` tokens they alias one-for-one (general-dentistry.css:46-62:
     `--f-display` → `--vs-font-display`, `--ink-soft` → `--vs-ink-soft`, …).
     Rendered values are unchanged. Resolved rather than kept, because the
     aliases are declared on `.gdent` — a band that reads them is a band that
     only works on one page (R3). */

  /* ── Band shell ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     general-dentistry.css:325-327 and :351-353.

     These two are the only rules here that tie with the page sheet
     (`.section` (0,2,0) vs `.gdent .section` (0,2,0); `.section.dark` (0,3,0)
     vs `.gdent .section.dark` (0,3,0)) and they are safe because the values
     are identical — checked, not assumed: every one of the twenty page sheets
     that defines `.section.dark` sets `background: var(--vs-sage-pale)`, and
     `.section` is `padding: 110px 0` on this page. A tie cannot be built on,
     but a tie between two identical values cannot be observed either. There is
     no descendant to root these at without inventing a wrapper element, and
     inventing one would change the markup this file exists to preserve. */
  .section {
    padding: 110px 0;
  }
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }

  /* No `scroll-margin-top` on `.section[id]`, unlike StatCalloutBlock:222.
     That block carries one because clear-aligners.css gives its sections one.
     general-dentistry.css gives it to `.hero` ONLY (:320-322) — `#trust` has
     never had one — so adding it here would silently change where the rail
     chip lands on a page this component is supposed to leave untouched. */

  .section .wrap {
    /* general-dentistry.css:66-70 */
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* ── The centred pull-quote ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     general-dentistry.css:671-679. Flex column with a 22px gap, which is why
     none of the children below carry vertical margins of their own. */
  .section .trust-quote {
    max-width: 780px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    text-align: center;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 22px;
  }

  /* Eyebrow. Type from general-dentistry.css:72-79, the hairline rules from
     :680-691.

     COLOUR IS NOT `.eyebrow.light`. The template writes `class="eyebrow light"`
     and `.gdent .eyebrow.light` (:80-82) sets `--vs-eyebrow-on-sage` — but
     `.gdent .section.dark .eyebrow` (:360-362) is (0,4,0) against its (0,3,0)
     and wins, so what actually renders on this band is `--vs-ink-soft`. The
     `light` class is kept in the markup because the diff is against the
     shipped HTML, not against what the class does. */
  .section .trust-quote .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  /* general-dentistry.css:685-691, with the hairline colour from :363-365
     (which wins there too, at (0,4,1) over (0,3,1) — same value either way). */
  .section .trust-quote .eyebrow::before,
  .section .trust-quote .eyebrow::after {
    content: '';
    width: 28px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* Heading. Type from general-dentistry.css:84-94 and :105-109, box from
     :692-695. Colour is ink, not the sage of the h1–h4 group rule: the sheet
     flips h2 to ink twice over, in `.gdent .section.dark h2` (:354-359) and
     again in its end-of-file override (:1369-1371). Collapsed to the value
     that renders. */
  .section .trust-quote h2 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  /* :global because the `<em>` arrives through set:html and carries no scope
     attribute — the same reason StatCalloutBlock.astro:239 reaches for it. The
     scope stays on `h2`, so this cannot leak off the band. Sage, not the
     sage-soft of the base `.gdent em` (:125-129): `.gdent .section.dark em`
     (:370-372) is (0,3,1) over its (0,1,1). */
  .section .trust-quote h2 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 300;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }

  /* First paragraph — the italic display pull-quote.
     general-dentistry.css:696-706.

     COLOUR IS INK-SOFT, NOT INK, and this is the trap in this band. The rule
     at :700 says `color: var(--vs-ink)`, but `.gdent .section.dark p`
     (:367-369) is (0,3,1) against `.gdent .trust-body`'s (0,2,0) and wins, so
     the shipped page renders #4a4a47 and not #2a2a28. Copying the declaration
     that is easier to find rather than the one that renders would darken this
     quote on a live page and look like a deliberate design change. Confirmed
     against the built page, not reasoned about — see the verification note in
     the batch report. */
  .section .trust-quote .trust-body {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.2vw, 26px);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    margin: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    max-width: 36ch;
  }
  /* general-dentistry.css:707-711, again with the colour that wins from
     :370-372 — the same sage the local rule asks for. :global for the same
     reason the heading's `<em>` needs it: `body2` arrives set:html. */
  .section .trust-quote .trust-body :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-style: italic;
  }
  /* Second paragraph — plain body copy, smaller and wider than the quote it
     follows. general-dentistry.css:712-721.

     Written as the adjacent-sibling rule the sheet uses rather than given its
     own class, because the class it does not have is the reason `body2` can
     never be styled as a quote by accident: with `body` blank there is no
     first sibling, and the surviving paragraph correctly renders as the
     italic pull-quote — which is the band a one-paragraph row should draw. */
  .section .trust-quote .trust-body + .trust-body {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-style: normal;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.4vw, 17px);
    line-height: 1.7;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  /* Attribution line. general-dentistry.css:722-734, plus `margin: 0` from the
     sheet's base `.gdent p` (:121-124) — the local rule never sets a margin,
     so leaving it out here would let a UA default reopen a gap the flex `gap`
     already accounts for. */
  .section .trust-quote .trust-attr {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: 8px;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  /* general-dentistry.css:735-739. Authored in this template, so it carries
     the scope attribute and needs no :global — unlike the `<em>`s above. */
  .section .trust-quote .trust-attr .stars {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-size: 16px;
  }

  /* Inline links inside the sage-pale band — general-dentistry.css:374-384.
     Carried even though the shipped copy has none: `body2` is an inline-HTML
     field, an editor adding an `<a class="vs-link">` is the expected use, and
     without this the link would fall back to the global `.vs-link` sage
     (global.css:39-49), which is the band's own background family and reads as
     unstyled text on it. :global for the same reason as the `<em>`s. */
  .section .trust-quote :global(.vs-link) {
    color: var(--vs-sage-deep);
    text-decoration-color: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section .trust-quote :global(.vs-link:hover),
  .section .trust-quote :global(.vs-link:focus-visible) {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    text-decoration-color: var(--vs-ink);
  }

  /* ── Responsive ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Re-united with the rules they modify rather than left in a trailing
     "responsive" section 600 lines away (3.2, step 3). The band has no
     breakpoint of its own — the two rules below are the page's shell
     breakpoint (general-dentistry.css:1296-1298 and :1318-1320), and the
     quote's own type is fluid via clamp() at every width. */
  @media (max-width: 780px) {
    .section {
      padding: 70px 0;
    }
    .section .wrap {
      padding: 0 22px;
    }
  }
</style>
